::ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
Visual reference: slope is rise divided by run between two points.
Find the slope through (1, 2) and (4, 11).
{
~A. 1/3#This reverses the change ratio. Use change in y over change in x.
=B. 3#Correct. (11 - 2)/(4 - 1) = 9/3 = 3.
~C. 9#This is the change in y only, not divided by the change in x.
~D. 13#This adds values instead of finding a rate of change.
}

::ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
Visual reference: slope is rise divided by run between two points.
Find the slope through (0, 9) and (3, 3).
{
~A. 2#The graph decreases, so the slope should be negative.
~B. -6#This is the change in y only, not divided by the change in x.
~C. 1/2#This reverses the ratio and misses the negative direction.
=D. -2#Correct. (3 - 9)/(3 - 0) = -6/3 = -2.
}

::ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
Visual reference: slope is rise divided by run between two points.
A table has outputs 4, 7, 10, 13 when inputs are 0, 1, 2, 3. What is the slope?
{
=A. 3#Correct. The output increases by 3 for each 1-unit input increase.
~B. 4#This is the starting output, not the slope.
~C. 7#This is one output value, not the repeated change.
~D. 13#This is the last output shown.
}

::ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
Visual reference: slope is rise divided by run between two points.
A cyclist travels 24 miles in 2 hours. What is the rate of change?
{
~A. 2 miles per hour#This is the time, not the distance per hour.
~B. 24 miles per hour#This uses the total distance without dividing by time.
=C. 12 miles per hour#Correct. 24 miles divided by 2 hours is 12 miles per hour.
~D. 48 miles per hour#This multiplies distance and time instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
Visual reference: slope is rise divided by run between two points.
What does a slope of 0 mean on a graph?
{
~A. The graph is vertical.#A vertical line has undefined slope, not zero slope.
=B. The y-value does not change as x changes.#Correct. A zero slope is a horizontal line.
~C. The x-value does not change as y changes.#That describes a vertical line.
~D. The graph is not a line.#A horizontal graph is still a line.
}

